A||N||9||SloPonti||rebuild 2.5 questions||||5
Z||000000||SloPonti||09-27-1999||09:57 PM||||i am currently rebuilding a 2.5 for my 84 coupe and the moter is being bored 40 over with new pistons,rings,comp cam,new high volume oil pump,new springs for head,and arp rod bolts! should i replace the rods themselves and if so what do you people reccommend? also what about pushrods and lifters! this car is a daily driver and is treated nicely but i do like to test the car to see what she will do! thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-28-1999||12:08 AM||||Who told you 40 over was safe with this block??????? It's not weak enough to start with??? find another block if this one is that bad. they are used in about a few million GM cars. No way I'd use this block punched more than 20. It's just not worth the trouble. Keep in mind these were built to be very cheap engines that are easy on gas.<P>As for a "normal" rebuild.... I'd put in a roller lifter cam set. Roller lifters Do Not work with a flat tappet cam. Rollers reduce cam load in this motor tremendously. They are why hardly any 86+ 4 cyls eat cam gears.<P>I'd definatly replace the rods. they are known for breaking, especially in the 84 motors. Basically, if you increase compression/hp which over boring is likely to do, and don't replace the original rods, you should expect to find a rod in your back in the not to distant future.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SloPonti||09-28-1999||06:10 PM||||this motor is not out of a fiero! it is out of a 84 - 86 clebrity and the gm spec book says it can be bored 10-20-30-40 and even 60 over and it will still be safe! my instrutor at my college were i am rebuilding the moter says that 40 over is no problem for this moter for it is strong enough! also were can i get rods for this moter!  i do not want remanufactored rods!  i want brand new ones!<BR>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eddie||09-28-1999||09:00 PM||||My 2.5 is 40 over whith hot cam,ported and polished cylinder head .The HEAD IS  shaved it also has a HOOKER header. It will run with a stock v6. 	All that work  and money and still <BR>some times the car will have some thing go wrong with it. Should have just got the v6 !!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||09-28-1999||10:31 PM||||I tend to agree with Ogre about being careful on how much you bore it out, although I wouldn't have a problem with a 0.030" overbore on a good block. BUT, 0.060" on a 2.5 liter and then claim it will be reliable? **shaking my head in disbelief** By the way, when rebuilding ANY engine, always bore it only the minumim needed. What tiny bit of power gained is not enough to justify weakening the block and causing it to run hotter because the water jacket is thinner. Besides, you may want to rebuild the engine again after years of future use.<P>The roller cam and lifters would be a great improvement. Will the roller lifters work in the older engines? I know on 350 V8 blocks, the engine is machined differently in order to run the roller cam setup. Although you can buy retrofit kits for the older small blocks, it's twice the cost. The rods are another weak point on the 2.5, trust me on this one. I had a rod let loose on my 87 not long ago.<P>Always replace flat hydraulic lifters during a high mileage rebuild or anytime the cam is replaced. I usually replace pushrods and rockers too, although some people will use the old ones, if they are in good condition. Inspect pushrods for straightness and the rockers for hairline stress cracks and wear. <BR>Valve springs are another corner many people cut, but if you're putting in a performance cam or plan on cranking this engine at higher than normal rpm, then you have to replace the springs with ones recommended to work with your cam. By the way, shop teachers are human too and don't always know everything about all engines.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SloPonti||09-29-1999||06:28 PM||||does anyone know were to get new rods then-i have decided to replace them and need new ones! i would like to stay away from remanufactured ones if at all possible! thanks for the help guys!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||09-29-1999||09:31 PM||||Give a couple of automotive machine shops a call and ask them about rod availability. I'd think they'd know if anyone. I'm sure you can still order them from GM too.||||reg||1
